9:["$","p",null,{"children":["That last property is what makes preemptible and spot instances viable, and it\nis the single largest cost lever available. The retry budget is\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"max_job_retries"}]," on the scheduler, defaulting to ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"3"}],"."]}]
a:["$","div",null,{"className":"my-6 flex gap-3 rounded-xl border px-5 py-4 border-amber-500/40 bg-amber-50/60 text-foreground dark:bg-amber-500/10","role":"alert","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"20","height":"20","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"1.75","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400","aria-hidden":"true","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"M12 9v4m0 4h.01M10.29 3.86 1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0Z"}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1 [&>p]:my-0 [&>p+p]:mt-2","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-1 font-mono text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em]","children":"`max_job_retries: 0` does not mean zero retries"}],["$","p",null,{"children":["A configured ",["$","code",null,{"children":"0"}]," is read as \"unset\" and falls back to the default of 3. There\nis no way to express \"never retry\" through this field."]}]]}]]}]
b:["$","p",null,{"children":["A worker is declared dead when it stops keepaliving for ",["$","code",null,{"children":"worker_timeout_s"}],",\nwhich defaults to ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"5 seconds"}]," and is checked once a second. That is a\ndeliberately short leash: fast detection means fast requeue, and the cost of a\nfalse positive is one repeated action rather than a stall."]}]
c:["$","$L6",null,{"as":"h2","id":"draining-for-the-shutdown-you-know-about","children":"Draining, for the shutdown you know about"}]
d:["$","p",null,{"children":"Retries handle the machines that die without warning. Machines that give you\nnotice deserve better, and there's a specific hook for it."}]
e:["$","p",null,{"children":"The admin interface exposes a drain endpoint:"}]
f:["$","$L3c",null,{"className":"shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark","style":{"--shiki-light":"#24292e","--shiki-dark":"#e1e4e8","--shiki-light-bg":"#fff","--shiki-dark-bg":"#24292e"},"tabIndex":"0","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M 6,1 C 4.354992,1 3,2.354992 3,4 v 16 c 0,1.645008 1.354992,3 3,3 h 12 c 1.645008,0 3,-1.354992 3,-3 V 8 7 A 1.0001,1.0001 0 0 0 20.707031,6.2929687 l -5,-5 A 1.0001,1.0001 0 0 0 15,1 h -1 z m 0,2 h 7 v 3 c 0,1.645008 1.354992,3 3,3 h 3 v 11 c 0,0.564129 -0.435871,1 -1,1 H 6 C 5.4358712,21 5,20.564129 5,20 V 4 C 5,3.4358712 5.4358712,3 6,3 Z M 15,3.4140625 18.585937,7 H 16 C 15.435871,7 15,6.5641288 15,6 Z\" fill=\"currentColor\" /></svg>","children":["$","$L3d",null,{"children":["$","code",null,{"children":["$","span",null,{"className":"line","children":["$","span",null,{"children":"POST {admin_path}/scheduler/{instance_name}/set_drain_worker/{worker_id}/1"}]}]}]}]}]
10:["$","p",null,{"children":"A draining worker accepts no new actions and finishes the ones it is running.\nThat is exactly the right response to a spot-termination notice or a rolling\nnode upgrade: call it when the notice arrives, and the in-flight work completes\ninstead of being thrown away and redone."}]
11:["$","a",null,{"href":"https://github.com/toxicwind/forge/blob/v1.6.5/forge-scheduler/src/worker.rs","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","data-source-link":"forge-scheduler/src/worker.rs","data-source-ref":"v1.6.5","title":"forge-scheduler/src/worker.rs at v1.6.5","className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1 rounded-md border border-border bg-surface-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 align-middle font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground no-underline transition-colors hover:border-brand/50 hover:text-brand","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"12","height":"12","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","aria-hidden":"true","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"m16 18 6-6-6-6M8 6l-6 6 6 6"}]}],"Worker"]}]
12:["$","p",null,{"children":"Nothing in the repository wires this to any cloud provider's interruption\nsignal. The handler is a short script you write, and it is the highest-value\npiece of glue on this page."}]
13:["$","$L6",null,{"as":"h2","id":"what-the-scheduler-remembers-and-what-it-forgets","children":"What the scheduler remembers, and what it forgets"}]
14:["$","p",null,{"children":"This is the constraint that shapes a hands-off design, so it's worth being\nprecise."}]
15:["$","p",null,{"children":["By default the scheduler keeps its queue ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"in process memory"}],". Restarting it\nloses the in-flight queue. Clients retry and the work gets done, so this is not\ndata loss in any lasting sense, but it does mean the scheduler is the one\ncomponent you cannot casually cycle under load, and that a single scheduler is a\nsingle point of interruption."]}]
16:["$","p",null,{"children":["A Redis-backed alternative exists, and the configuration names it honestly:\nthe field is ",["$","code",null,{"children":"experimental_backend"}],". It moves queue state out of the process so\nschedulers stop being special. It also carries the rough edges the name implies:\nthere's a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"fallback_match_interval_s"}]," in the config specifically to paper over\neventually-consistent searches."]}]
17:["$","div",null,{"className":"my-6 flex gap-3 rounded-xl border px-5 py-4 border-amber-500/40 bg-amber-50/60 text-foreground dark:bg-amber-500/10","role":"alert","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"20","height":"20","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"1.75","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400","aria-hidden":"true","children":"$a:props:children:0:props:children"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1 [&>p]:my-0 [&>p+p]:mt-2","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-1 font-mono text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em]","children":"The Redis backend does not emit the queue metric"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","code",null,{"children":"execution.active.count"}],", the signal an autoscaler needs, is emitted only by\nthe in-memory backend. The Redis path emits none of it, so the topology that\nmakes schedulers replaceable is also the topology where the scaling signal\ndisappears. Know this before you plan around it."]}]]}]]}]
18:["$","$L6",null,{"as":"h2","id":"the-signal-you-scale-on","children":"The signal you scale on"}]
19:["$","p",null,{"children":["An autoscaler needs a number that says \"more work is waiting than there is\ncapacity for.\" That number is queue depth: actions sitting in the ",["$","code",null,{"children":"queued"}],"\nstage rather than ",["$","code",null,{"children":"executing"}],"."]}]
1a:["$","p",null,{"children":["Forge emits it as ",["$","code",null,{"children":"execution.active.count"}],", an up-down counter attributed\nby ",["$","code",null,{"children":"execution.stage"}],"."]}]
1b:["$","a",null,{"href":"https://github.com/toxicwind/forge/blob/v1.6.5/forge-util/src/metrics.rs","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","data-source-link":"forge-util/src/metrics.rs","data-source-ref":"v1.6.5","title":"forge-util/src/metrics.rs at v1.6.5","className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1 rounded-md border border-border bg-surface-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 align-middle font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground no-underline transition-colors hover:border-brand/50 hover:text-brand","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"12","height":"12","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","aria-hidden":"true","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"m16 18 6-6-6-6M8 6l-6 6 6 6"}]}],"EXECUTION_METRICS"]}]
1c:["$","p",null,{"children":"Three things about that metric decide your architecture, and all three surprise\npeople:"}]
1d:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Telemetry is OTLP push, not Prometheus pull."}]," The binary has no ",["$","code",null,{"children":"/metrics"}],"\nendpoint to scrape. It pushes OpenTelemetry to a collector, and the collector is\nwhat a Prometheus-shaped world reads from.\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"deployment-examples/metrics/"}]," is the working example of that pipeline."]}]
1e:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Queue depth is a derived series, not a metric name."}]," What you scale on is a\nrecording rule that sums the counter filtered to the ",["$","code",null,{"children":"queued"}]," stage. The\nexample rules in ",["$","code",null,{"children":"deployment-examples/metrics/prometheus-recording-rules.yml"}],"\ndefine ",["$","code",null,{"children":"forge:queue_depth"}],", ",["$","code",null,{"children":"forge:worker_utilization"}]," and\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"forge:actions_per_worker"}],". An HPA reads the recording rule through an\nexternal-metrics adapter; there is no metric the binary emits that an HPA can\ntarget directly."]}]
1f:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"The metrics module is Business Source Licensed."}]," ",["$","code",null,{"children":"forge-util/src/metrics.rs"}],"\ncarries a header requiring an enterprise license agreement, unlike the\nFunctional Source License covering most of the repository. Production\nobservability is exactly the case that header describes, so settle the licensing\nquestion before you design around it."]}]
20:["$","div",null,{"className":"my-6 flex gap-3 rounded-xl border px-5 py-4 border-brand/40 bg-brand-soft/40 text-foreground","role":"note","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"20","height":"20","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"1.75","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-brand","aria-hidden":"true","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"M12 16v-4M12 8h.01M12 22c5.523 0 10-4.477 10-10S17.523 2 12 2 2 6.477 2 12s4.477 10 10 10Z"}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1 [&>p]:my-0 [&>p+p]:mt-2","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-1 font-mono text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em]","children":"Workers emit no metrics of their own"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Every utilization number is inferred scheduler-side from what it knows about\nits workers. No worker-side telemetry exists to scrape, so host-level CPU\nand memory from your existing node monitoring is the complement to the\nscheduler's view, not a duplicate of it."}]]}]]}]
21:["$","$L6",null,{"as":"h2","id":"what-the-repository-ships-and-what-it-doesnt","children":"What the repository ships, and what it doesn't"}]
22:["$","p",null,{"children":"Being direct, because this is the part most likely to be assumed:"}]
23:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"The repository contains no autoscaling manifests for Forge."}]," The Helm\ncharts for Kubernetes ship with ",["$","$L7",null,{"href":"https://enterprise.forge.com","children":"Forge Enterprise"}],",\nnot with this repository. The Kustomize building blocks under ",["$","code",null,{"children":"kubernetes/"}],"\nset fixed replica counts. No HorizontalPodAutoscaler for workers, no KEDA ",["$","code",null,{"children":"ScaledObject"}],",\nno cluster-autoscaler or Karpenter configuration, and no node pools or\ntolerations for build workers. The one HPA in the tree scales the OpenTelemetry\ncollector, not Forge."]}]
24:["$","p",null,{"children":"What the repository does give you is the harder half: a worker that is safe to\nkill, a scheduler that requeues what it loses, a drain hook for planned\nshutdown, a queue-depth signal, and a working metrics pipeline to carry it."}]
25:["$","p",null,{"children":"What you assemble is the standard Kubernetes half: an HPA on the derived queue\nmetric via an external-metrics adapter, a cluster autoscaler or Karpenter for\nthe nodes underneath, spot node pools with the drain hook wired to interruption\nnotices, and node affinity for heterogeneous pools. These are not Forge\nconcepts, which is the point: nothing here needs a bespoke controller."}]
26:["$","div",null,{"className":"my-6 flex gap-3 rounded-xl border px-5 py-4 border-brand/40 bg-brand-soft/40 text-foreground","role":"note","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"20","height":"20","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"1.75","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-brand","aria-hidden":"true","children":"$20:props:children:0:props:children"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1 [&>p]:my-0 [&>p+p]:mt-2","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-1 font-mono text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em]","children":"Where the manifests are"}],["$","p",null,{"children":["Kubernetes deployments, including the Helm charts, are part of\n",["$","$L7",null,{"href":"https://enterprise.forge.com","children":"Forge Enterprise"}],". This repository ships\nonly the Kustomize building blocks under ",["$","code",null,{"children":"kubernetes/"}]," that its own examples\nuse, and no KEDA adapter."]}]]}]]}]
27:["$","$L6",null,{"as":"h2","id":"what-no-human-in-the-loop-actually-means","children":"What \"no human in the loop\" actually means"}]
28:["$","p",null,{"children":"A realistic end state, in the order the pieces earn their keep:"}]
29:["$","p",null,{"children":"Workers scale on queue depth and fall to a small floor overnight. Most of the\nfleet is preemptible, because retries make interruption a cost rather than an\nincident, and the drain hook converts warned shutdowns into clean ones. Node\ncapacity follows pod demand through your existing autoscaler. The CAS scales\nseparately and more conservatively, because it holds the state everything else\ndepends on. Alerts fire on the things automation cannot resolve (sustained\nqueue depth that scaling isn't clearing, error rates, storage approaching its\nlimit) rather than on capacity, which now handles itself."}]
2a:["$","p",null,{"children":"The queue-depth alert is the one to get right. It's the signal that your\nautoscaler has hit a ceiling it cannot pass, whether that's a quota, a node\npool's maximum, or an exhausted spot market. Everything else is noise by\ncomparison."}]
2b:["$","$L6",null,{"as":"h2","id":"common-questions","children":"Common questions"}]
2c:["$","$L3e",null,{"children":[["$","$L3f",null,{"title":"Can I autoscale the CAS the same way?","id":"can-i-autoscale-the-cas-the-same-way","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["More carefully. Workers are stateless and cheap to churn; the CAS holds the data\neverything else is asking for. Stateless CAS replicas in front of one shared\nbacking store scale on request rate, but a local filesystem tier is warm state\nthat a new replica does not have, so scaling in throws away cache locality.\nScale it slowly, and prefer bigger replicas over more of them.\n",["$","$L7",null,{"href":"/operate/tuning","children":"Tuning"}]," covers the storage path."]}]}],["$","$L3f",null,{"title":"What happens to an action when its worker is preempted?","id":"what-happens-to-an-action-when-its-worker-is-preempted","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["The scheduler notices the worker stop keepaliving within ",["$","code",null,{"children":"worker_timeout_s"}],",\nincrements the action's attempt count, clears its worker affinity, and requeues\nit. Any worker can pick it up. Once the attempt count passes ",["$","code",null,{"children":"max_job_retries"}],"\nthe action completes with an error saying it attempted to execute too many\ntimes, so a systematically failing action does not requeue forever."]}]}],["$","$L3f",null,{"title":"Is one scheduler enough?","id":"is-one-scheduler-enough","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["For most deployments, yes: the scheduler does bookkeeping, not the expensive\nwork, and it fails over by restarting. The reasons to want more are availability\nacross zones and not wanting a restart to drop the in-flight queue. Both point\nat the Redis backend, whose name says ",["$","code",null,{"children":"experimental"}]," and which stops emitting the\nqueue metric. That trade is the current state of the art here."]}]}],["$","$L3f",null,{"title":"How aggressively should workers scale in?","id":"how-aggressively-should-workers-scale-in","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"Less aggressively than they scale out. Scaling out has a bounded cost, a\nmachine you briefly didn't need. Scaling in during a lull that ends thirty\nseconds later costs a cold start on every action that arrives next. Generous\nscale-up, patient scale-down."}]}],["$","$L3f",null,{"title":"Do I need Prometheus, or can I use my existing vendor?","id":"do-i-need-prometheus-or-can-i-use-my-existing-vendor","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"Anything that ingests OTLP works, and the collector can fan out to several\ndestinations at once. Prometheus appears throughout the examples because the\nrecording rules and the HPA external-metrics adapter are expressed in it, but the\nrequirement is OTLP ingestion plus something an autoscaler can query, not\nPrometheus specifically."}]}]]}]
